put('figures/figure_legends.md',f'''# Figure legends (author review)

**Figure 1. Performance and numerical validation of CellChatESS.** Except for the explicitly archived biological illustrations in D, E and J, the quantitative results use the pinned CellChat 2.2.0.9001 baseline, CellChatESS 0.2.2 and, where indicated, AutoZyme 0.3.1. All primary performance comparisons use one thread per method in separate R processes. (A) Inference-call runtimes for Original CellChat and ESS across 12 datasets, six target sizes and three runs (216 paired runs; 129 distinct prepared inputs). Points are actual measurements; circles and error bars show medians and interquartile ranges. Both external timers span the complete inference call, including internal conversion and initialization, and exclude process startup, package/input loading, output serialization and downstream analysis. The median paired Original/ESS ratio is {p['pooled_median']:.2f}× (95% dataset-cluster percentile bootstrap CI, {ci[0]:.2f}–{ci[1]:.2f}×; 10,000 resamples). Repeated sizes/runs are not independent biological replicates. (B) Paired inference-call times for AutoZyme and ESS on 32 inputs from 12 datasets, with all six method orders (192 blocks). Symbols identify input strata; the dashed line is equality. The pooled paired AutoZyme/ESS ratio is {a['pooled_median']:.3f}×; ESS is faster in {a['ess_faster_blocks']} blocks. (C) Six-stage controlled ablation, with three timing repeats per stage: A0, Original CellChat; A1, dense Rcpp with all signaling genes; A2, dense Rcpp with projected genes; A3, on-demand aggregation without caching or pruning; A4, cached ESS without pruning; A5, full ESS. Lines connect stage medians for three different datasets (10X 1k, EGAS00001004422 10k and PRJNA638151 50k), not a within-dataset cell-count scaling series. A3 is slower than the dense path; caching removes repeated aggregation, and pruning yields a further reduction. These results do not establish independent benefits for every ESS-specific optimization. (D,E) Preserved aggregate communication networks for the representative glioblastoma sample (K = 9), from archived Original and accelerated outputs. The accelerated saved object is identified as sparse_stream; its exact package version/commit is unconfirmed. These panels illustrate the archived biological result and are not attributed to the newly timed final candidate. (F) Probability agreement for a newly run 10X 1k example (K = 12; maximum absolute difference {S['representative']['max_abs_difference']:.2e}). Across all 216 new pairs, the maximum absolute probability difference is 1.11 × 10⁻¹⁶; empirical p-value tensors and default aggregate counts are elementwise identical. Probability tensors agree to floating-point precision and are not bitwise identical. (G) Full-process peak RSS for the three methods in 192 blocks, with actual measurements, medians and IQRs. (H) Within-block ESS minus AutoZyme peak RSS; ESS is lower in 192/192 blocks. (I) Absolute resource measurements for the full 627-LR Xenium inference (1,156,091 cells; 2,357 non-spatial grid groups). Input-object and dense-output bytes are distinguished from process RSS. No newly paired Original Xenium timing is available, so no Xenium relative speedup is claimed. (J) Preserved ligand–receptor bubble results from the same archived glioblastoma outputs as D/E; original interactions, groups and ordering are retained. Bubble sizes follow the archived visualization score (−1/log probability); colorbar ticks express communication probability through the corresponding inverse transformation. Points shown are the archived filtered set, not a new biological analysis.

**Figure R1. Dataset-level performance, pruning and time-resolved Xenium resources.** All newly measured panels use CellChat 2.2.0.9001 and the final ESS 0.2.2 candidate or explicitly identified controlled/instrumented derivatives. (A) Dataset-specific descriptive distributions of the 216 paired Original/ESS inference-call ratios, sorted by dataset median. Boxes show the median and IQR; whiskers extend to the most extreme observation within 1.5 IQR, and all real observations are overlaid with small vertical offsets. Repeated runs and scales are nested within datasets. The pooled median is {p['pooled_median']:.2f}×; the {ci[0]:.2f}–{ci[1]:.2f}× interval is obtained by resampling datasets with all of their records, not from the boxplot whiskers. (B) Medians of the within-block AutoZyme 0.3.1/ESS ratios for each of 12 datasets, based on 192 blocks on 32 inputs. The dashed line denotes equal time. The median of the 12 dataset medians is {a['median_of_dataset_medians']:.3f}× (95% dataset-bootstrap percentile CI {aci[0]:.3f}–{aci[1]:.3f}×; 100,000 resamples). This statistic and interval are distinct from the pooled block median of {a['pooled_median']:.3f}×. (C) Three alternating-order A4/A5 paired runs on the maximum-retained-fraction SmartSeq2 1k input (prepared replicate 2; retained LR-specific work {frac:.3f}%). Bars show real inference-call measurements; pairwise ratios are {rat}×, with a median of {S['high_retention_median_speedup']:.2f}×. A4 caches on-demand aggregation but disables pruning; A5 is full ESS. In this tested input, the controlled implementations preserve p values and default counts relative to Original; probability differences are recorded separately. Earlier runs on prepared replicate 1 ({8.6445556792:.3f}% retained work) remain separately archived and are not relabeled as the maximum-retention input. (D) Full sampled process RSS and a local enlargement around output completion. Time zero is immediately before the inference call; negative times represent process preparation. Markers and connecting straight segments show measured samples, with a median interval of {x['median_sampling_interval_sec']:.3f}s and maximum gap {x['max_sampling_gap_sec']:.3f}s; no values are imputed. Vertical dashed lines mark instrumented C++ computation completion and R function return. The zoom interval is selected from these actual event timestamps, rather than retained at the historical 30–34 min window. GNU time peak RSS is {x['peak_RSS_GiB']:.6f}GiB; the independently sampled maximum is {x['sampled_peak_RSS_GiB']:.6f}GiB. Inference-call time is {float(x['elapsed_sec']):.3f}s and full-process time is 25:27.48. Each dense output has a 25.95GiB double payload (51.90GiB combined), whereas the signaling input object occupies 0.894GiB. Exit status is zero. Stage RSS locates the rise in process memory but does not uniquely assign allocations to individual R objects. The full profile is an absolute ESS resource measurement; five-target bootstrap tracing does not establish Original equivalence for all 627 LR interactions.

**Alt text, Figure 1.** A ten-panel figure shows new single-thread runtimes, controlled ablation, probability agreement and memory benchmarks, alongside explicitly archived glioblastoma networks and ligand–receptor bubbles. ESS reduces inference time and RSS while p values and default aggregate counts match in the tested paired queues.

**Alt text, Figure R1.** Dataset speedup boxplots and bars show heterogeneous gains; paired pruning bars show measured A4/A5 times. A measured Xenium RSS trace rises during output completion and reaches approximately112GiB.
''')
